 spread out to cool on a baking sheet.";}i:2;i:2565;}i:164;a:3:{i:0;s:17:"listcontent_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:2724;}i:165;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"listitem_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:2724;}i:166;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"listitem_open";i:1;a:1:{i:0;i:1;}i:2;i:2724;}i:167;a:3:{i:0;s:16:"listcontent_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:2724;}i:168;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:135:" Add the broccoli rabe and cook until just wilted, 1 minute, then drain in a colander. Rinse with cool water, then squeeze into a ball.";}i:2;i:2728;}i:169;a:3:{i:0;s:17:"listcontent_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:2863;}i:170;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"listitem_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:2863;}i:171;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"listitem_open";i:1;a:1:{i:0;i:1;}i:2;i:2863;}i:172;a:3:{i:0;s:16:"listcontent_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:2863;}i:173;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:188:" Put cauliflower and broccoli rabe into the empty pasta pot. Add garlic, rosemary and red-pepper flakes, and stir together. Season with salt and pepper, and drizzle with 2 tablespoons oil.";}i:2;i:2867;}i:174;a:3:{i:0;s:17:"listcontent_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3055;}i:175;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"listitem_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3055;}i:176;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"listitem_open";i:1;a:1:{i:0;i:1;}i:2;i:3055;}i:177;a:3:{i:0;s:16:"listcontent_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3055;}i:178;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:144:" Add the béchamel and the rigatoni. Mix well with the cauliflower and broccoli rabe. Add the provolone and Pecorino Romano, and mix well again.";}i:2;i:3059;}i:179;a:3:{i:0;s:17:"listcontent_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3203;}i:180;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"listitem_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3203;}i:181;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"listitem_open";i:1;a:1:{i:0;i:1;}i:2;i:3203;}i:182;a:3:{i:0;s:16:"listcontent_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3203;}i:183;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:319:" Heat oven to 400 degrees. Pile the pasta mixture into an oiled 9-by-13-inch baking dish. Bake until nicely browned and crisp on top, about 40 minutes. Leave to rest for 10 minutes before serving. (Alternatively, refrigerate the assembled dish for up to 6 hours and bake later. Bring to room temperature before baking.)";}i:2;i:3207;}i:184;a:3:{i:0;s:17:"listcontent_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3526;}i:185;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"listitem_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3526;}i:186;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"listitem_open";i:1;a:1:{i:0;i:1;}i:2;i:3526;}i:187;a:3:{i:0;s:16:"listcontent_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3526;}i:188;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:207:" To serve, put a large spoonful of pasta into individual bowls or plates, making sure each portion has some of the crisp top. Spoon some tomato sauce around each portion and sprinkle with more grated cheese.";}i:2;i:3530;}i:189;a:3:{i:0;s:17:"listcontent_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3737;}i:190;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"listitem_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3737;}i:191;a:3:{i:0;s:11:"listo_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3737;}i:192;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3737;}i:193;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:240:"Notes: Max Alexander, Rome, Contestant MasterChef Italia 2020-20212 days ago
1. Don’t fuss with a béchamel, just use cream. (The extra calories, if you worry about such things, are reduced by the elimination of the oil to make the roux.)";}i:2;i:3740;}i:194;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3980;}i:195;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3980;}i:196;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:283:"2. Why use whole canned tomatoes if you’re going to pass the sauce through a blender? Just use every Italian grandmother’s favorite sauce base: Pomì tomato purée (called “pomodoro passato” here), available in every American supermarket. Save the blender for daiquiri night.";}i:2;i:3982;}i:197;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4265;}i:198;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4265;}i:199;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:567:"This baked pasta — please don’t call it a “pasta bake” — is a luscious affair, with two sauces. A creamy white béchamel is employed to toss with the pasta and vegetables. When it emerges, bubbly and bronzed and crisp on top, a bright, light tomato sauce adorns each serving. (If preferred, you can layer both sauces instead.) Putting it together is somewhat like building a lasagna — a bit of a fussy project — but once assembled, it's no trouble at all to bake and serve. Prepare it all several hours in advance, then pop it in the oven when you like.";}i:2;i:4269;}i:200;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4269;}i:201;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"document_end";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4269;}}